defined basins so they're they're  already delineated by the National  Weather Service and whichever Basin got  the most rain that's the one that I  consider is the contractor that  contributing watershed for the flood so  then I used ArcGIS hydrology tools and a  10 meter DM to delineate the watershed  that contributes to each blood and if  you're familiar with with flow  accumulation  algorithms then I just you be might be  interested to know that our GIS uses an  eight direction algorithm so it assumes  that waterfalls it can flow and one of  it can flow like in the basically the  compass directions ok so for soil  properties I used mostly the sir go soil  database so i got porosity calculated  based on both density the saturated  hydraulic conductivity and all of these  cell properties were averaged over the  watershed and then for soil moisture i  used a land surface model that accounts  for precipitation evaporation soil  properties transpiration and then  calculates what the model is what the  soil moisture would be on a given day  for a four kilometre grid and i use the  top ten centimeters and we consider that  sort of the most hydrologically active  area for the atmosphere the storm  properties we used a radar product that  aggregates rainfall in data into these  polygons that have that are their  hydrologic polygons so they're based on  on actual land surface elevation and we  were able to extract the intensity so  for for my purposes i counted the  maximum five minute rain rate anywhere  in the watershed so even if it rained  you know without looking over that  spatial and temporal variability  whatever the most intense rainfall was  that's the one that i considered and  then similarly wherever in the watershed  there was the most accumulation of  precipitation so a depth we talk about  precipitation and inches or millimeters  wherever that was the highest that was  what I considered the amount of  precipitation for that flood event and  it's sort of it's worth noting that as  an exercise I did do a more detailed  temporal and spatial analysis or model  of the two different runoff types so for  infiltration excess I used to green in  amped equations and said for every 10  meter square every 10 meter grid cell in  a raster what was the sum of the excess  rainfall over the infiltration capacity  summed up over the whole storm and what  would be the the total infiltration  excess runoff from that that calculation  and for saturation I used a model based  on the curve number to say the same  thing for every 10 meter cell for the  whole watershed what was the sum of the  saturation excess runoff based on soil  moisture storage and the rainfall depth  this took me a couple of months to get  all of the products all of the inputs  together for this one watershed so I  needed a method that was less  computationally and and sort of my time  intensive to talk about the different  runoff mechanisms and the flash floods  and our hundred and eighty flood data  set so I came up with these indices  where basically for infiltration XS I  said if the maximum rainfall rate  anywhere in the watershed exceeds the  average case at then saturated hydraulic  conductivity then we're going to call  that the rate ratio so the excess of  rain rate over soil infiltration  capacity which I know saturated  hydraulic conductivity does not equal  soil infiltration capacity but I'm using  it as a proxy similarly if the rainfall  depth exceeds the soil moisture deficit  that's going to be our depth ratio so  the rain depth over the soil moisture  deficit  it and I calculated the soil moisture  deficit as the soil moisture the day  before the event subtracted from the  total soil porosity and then times the  depth of the soil to bedrock or  permanent water table so the results of  these analyses you can see the  variability in the landscape this is the  these histograms are my data set so  before I showed you a map of the whole  northeast this is what my dataset looks  like so the impervious area was  generally less than ten percent slope  was pretty variable but you know around  ten percent was the was the sort of  typical value saturated hydraulic  conductivity typically right around  between fifty and a hundred millimeters  per hour and soil depth right around a  meter typically but some variation in  those and then also quite a bit of  variation in the storm property so again  this is the maximum rate and the maximum  depth for the storm and you can see that  most of the storms were pretty intense  100 millimetres an hour but lots of them  were not very intense so that's  something to keep in mind as we talk  about what's causing flash floods in  this region and I wanted to sort of  point out for for you know reference the  storms in Ithaca so every year we would  expect to see a storm that's around 85  millimeters per hour at some point in  the storm and a total daily accumulation  of about 50 millimeters so that's normal  for a year so just to give you a  reference of where these storms fall  with respect to a normal year so when I  looked at the rain ratio of the rate  ratio and the depth ratio I came up with  these quadrants so basically if the rate  ratio is greater than one I would  expect to see some infiltration excess  runoff and if the cetera if the depth  ratio was greater than one I would  expect to see some saturation excess run  off and then we have these other  quadrants where maybe both runoff  mechanisms are happening and then this  is a very interesting quadrant because  we can't identify either mechanism  happening by my methods but I have some  ideas about why things might be flooding  down in that quadrant so this is how it  breaks down about half of the events  were infiltration excess and  thirty-seven percent can't identify and  three percent saturation excess thirteen  percent both mechanisms are happening so  the spatial distribution of these  mechanisms or combinations of mechanis