nd  municipal storm water systems and one of  my research projects was looking deeper  into one aspect of this infrastructure  which is called Road culverts and I I  looked at a whole bunch of culverts  in several watersheds in the Hudson  River Valley and I said what's the  capacity of this culvert how much water  can this culvert handle it at its  maximum flow rate and then what storm  would produce that much rainfall too  meta to max out that culverts capacity  oh it's a barrel like metal or plastic  barrel that goes under the road so you  have a ditch an open ditch and then a  pipe and then an open ditch on the other  side so it's like so it serves to get  the water under the road and then it's  an open ditch on the other side so in a  city everything would be underground but  in more rural areas we just have these  open ditches and then the culvert gets  the water under the road okay so we  looked at the capacity of these culverts  how much water can it convey at its  maximum and then which storm return  period would would exceed meet or exceed  that capacity and then i color-coded  them because people like to see simple  ways of visualizing the the results of  this so I actually gave these maps to  the cities or towns that that were in or  close to these watersheds and I said  these dark red culverts are your problem  areas these are the ones that are going  to flood the most frequently and you can  see from this map that quite a few of  them are in the orange and red range  which means that they're likely to be  inundated or exceeded every one or two  years so that sounds like a lot it seems  like a lot of failure and it turns out  that this is actually verified by the  people who live there they say yeah this  culvert backs up Bloods the road every  year or every other year  so it seems kind of extreme but it's  true in some places anyway that these  culverts aren't actually designed for  the amount of runoff that's reaching  them and we don't have good records of  every time this culvert floods because  people only report it if it's extreme or  if they notice or if they care so I know  here in the Ithaca area I talked to  people from Lansing who say their  driveway floods every year or two years  or the ditch fills up and almost hits  the road every year or two years so  we're kind of right on the cusp of  infrastructure failures really  frequently in in a lot of different  places so what can we do about it we can  first of all we can get people to report  these floods or culvert failures more  frequently and that would maybe look  like smartphone pictures uploaded and  sent to the National Weather Service  maybe geotagged so that we'd know  exactly where it was it could be more  like an app where people can you know  put in their intersection where they are  and say no there was a flood here on  this day at this time if we had more  detailed information about this this is  my this is my sort of engineering  remotely calculated way of saying which  culverts are vulnerable but if we could  have actual records of which culverts  are flooding that would be even better  and then we could give that information  to the city planners and they could  decide if they have a little money which  they usually don't but let's say they  get some they can prioritize which  culverts to repair or or upgrade so I  guess the bad news is that it's not  going to get any better so we're  expecting more intense rainfall in the  Northeast and a lot of places in the  world  and rare storms so I already said that  these flush floods are happening because  of not very rare storms but rare storms  are becoming less rare so we're going to  see even more flash floods if my  assessments of why these things are  happening are true and we're seeing that  in a lot of places our infrastructure is  not adequate and it's going to be even  worse as we get more and more rainfall  either more intense or more total  accumulation so maybe that leads to more  f